abstract:
arXiv:2611.07972v1 Announce Type: new 
Abstract: We prove unconditionally that either NE contains a language requiring Boolean circuits of size at least $2^n/(2n)$, or $VP^0_{\mathbb{Z}}
e VNP^0_{\mathbb{Z}}$, a constant-free version of Valiant's conjecture over the integers. The Boolean alternative implies that $NP
ot\subseteq SIZE[n^k]$ for every constant $k\ge1$. Our proof gives a fine-grained variant of the connection between Polynomial Identity Testing (PIT) and circuit lower bounds established by Kabanets and Impagliazzo (2004). We show that the specific identity tests required by this argument can be performed over the integers without a derandomization assumption.
  Over a fixed finite field $\mathbb{F}$, we first give a deterministic $O(st^{d-1})$-time algorithm for PIT on size-$s$ circuits computing degree-$d$ polynomials in $t$ variables. We further show that, for any fixed $d\ge2$ and $\varepsilon>0$, improving this running time by a factor of $t^{\varepsilon}$, even nondeterministically, implies either the same Boolean lower bound or an arithmetic circuit lower bound of $t^{1+\varepsilon/2}$ for a polynomial family from $VNP_{\mathbb{F}}$. If nondeterministic PIT instead runs in time $s^{o(d)}t^{(1-\varepsilon)d}$ for infinitely many degrees $d$, then the arithmetic lower bound strengthens to $VP_{\mathbb{F}}
e VNP_{\mathbb{F}}$. Over the integers, we give a deterministic PIT algorithm for syntactically set-multilinear circuits running in time $s^{O(1)}t^{5d/6}$, which yields the unconditional result above.
  Combining the Kabanets-Impagliazzo argument with range avoidance, we show that an NP algorithm for PIT over $\mathbb{Z}$ gives a purely arithmetic win-win: either $VP_{\mathbb{Z}}
e VNP_{\mathbb{Z}}$, or constant-degree polynomials of maximum circuit complexity can be constructed in FNP.
